orks for n=3.\n\nBut maybe the problem requires that the lines are distinct and also that each line must contain at least two of the points? No such requirement. The problem only says there exist n distinct lines such that for all positive integers a,b with a+b ≤ n+1, the point (a,b) is on at least one of the lines. It does not require each line to contain at least one of those points? Actually each line could be arbitrary, but they must be distinct. So we can have a line that contains no required points, but that would be wasteful. But it's allowed. So we can always add extra lines that are irrelevant. So for any n, we could have k=n (all lines sunny) by just taking n arbitrary sunny lines, but they must cover all points. So we need to ensure coverage. But we can always add extra lines that are irrelevant as long as we have enough lines to cover all points. So for n=3, we can have 2 sunny lines and 1 vertical line covering all points. So k=2 is possible for n=3. But the solution says k=2 doesn't work for any n≥3. That seems false.\n\nBut maybe the problem expects that the lines must be distinct and also that each line must be used to cover at least one of the required points? The problem statement does not explicitly require each line to contain at least one of the points. However, typical combinatorial geometry problems often assume that lines are used to cover points, but they don't forbid lines that cover no points. But it's unnatural to include lines that cover no points because they would be wasted. But the condition is \"there exist n distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following: for all positive integers a and b with a+b ≤ n+1, the point (a,b) is on at least one of the lines; and exactly k of the lines are sunny.\" It does not say each line must contain at least one of the points. So we could have lines that are irrelevant. So for any n, we could have k=n (all lines sunny) by taking n arbitrary sunny lines that collectively cover all points. But