s a third sunny line, contradicting k=2.\" Let's examine that reasoning.\n\nWe need to understand the geometry: Points are arranged in columns (x coordinate). For each x = i, the points are (i, b) where b ranges from 1 to n+1-i. So column i has n+1-i points. For i = n-2, column has n+1-(n-2) = 3 points: (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). For i = n-1, column has 2 points: (n-1,1),(n-1,2). For i = n, column has 1 point: (n,1). So the solution says column x=n-2 has 3 points, forcing a vertical line. Let's examine: Could we cover those 3 points with non-vertical lines? Possibly we could use a line of slope -1: x+y = constant. For column x=n-2, points have coordinates (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). Their sums are (n-1),(n),(n+1). So they lie on three different lines of slope -1: x+y = n-1, n, n+1. So a single slope -1 line cannot cover more than one of them. Horizontal lines cannot cover more than one because they have same y coordinate? Actually (n-2,1) and (n-2,2) have different y, so horizontal lines cannot cover both. So the only way to cover all three points with a single line is vertical line x=n-2. But we have only n lines total, and we need to cover all points. Could we use multiple lines to cover those three points? Possibly we could use two lines: one vertical line covering two of them and another line covering the third. But the solution says \"forcing a vertical line\". Let's examine: Since we have only n lines, we need to cover all points. The column n-2 has 3 points. If we don't use a vertical line for that column, we would need at least 2 lines to cover those 3 points (since each non-vertical line can cover at most 2 points in that column? Actually a non-vertical line can intersect a given vertical line at most once, so it can cover at most one point in that column. So to cover 3 points in column n-2 without a vertical line, we would need at least 3 lines (one for each point). But we have only n lines total, and we need to cover many columns. So using a vertical line for 