y line connecting (n,1) to a point in column n-1 is either horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny. So a sunny line cannot cover (n,1) together with any point in column n-1. So to cover (n,1) with a sunny line, it must be alone (i.e., the line passes only through (n,1) among required points). But we have only k sunny lines, and we want k=2. So we have at most 2 sunny lines. One of them could be used to cover (n,1) alone. The other sunny line could be used elsewhere. But we also have the vertical line x=n-2 already used. So we have n-2 lines left (including possibly other non-sunny lines). Let's see if we can cover all points with exactly 2 sunny lines.\n\nWe need to cover column n-1 (two points) and column n (one point) and all earlier columns. The vertical line x=n-2 covers its three points. The remaining points are in columns 1 to n-3 (which have many points) plus column n-1 (two points) and column n (one point). We have n-2 lines left, of which exactly 2 are sunny, and the rest (n-4) are non-sunny. So we need to cover the rest with n-2 lines.\n\nWe need to see if it's possible to cover column n-1 and column n with the remaining lines while using only 2 sunny lines. Since (n,1) cannot be paired with any point in column n-1 on a sunny line, we must either use a non-sunny line to cover (n,1) (vertical x=n, horizontal y=1, or slope -1 line x+y=n+1). But vertical x=n is non-sunny, horizontal y=1 is non-sunny, slope -1 line x+y=n+1 passes through (n,1) and (n-1,2) and (n-2,3) etc. Actually x+y=n+1 includes (n,1) and (n-1,2) and (n-2,3) etc. So that line is non-sunny. So we could cover (n,1) with a non-sunny line, perhaps x+y=n+1, which also covers (n-1,2) and (n-2,3). But (n-2,3) is already covered by vertical line x=n-2. That's okay. So we could use a non-sunny line x+y=n+1 to cover (n,1) and (n-1,2). Then (n-1,1) remains uncovered. That could be covered by a sunny line alone (or by a non-sunny line). But we have only n-4 non-sunny lines left (since we used vertical x=