pe = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, sunny. (1,3) with (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. That uses three sunny lines, each covering two points. So we have a configuration with exactly 3 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line. So k=3 works for n=4.\n\nThus answer {0,1,3} seems plausible for all n≥3.\n\nNow we need to verify if there are any other possible k values for some n. Could k be n? That would be all lines sunny. But we have n lines covering n(n+1)/2 points. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, so each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. Since there are n columns (x=1,...,n), each sunny line can cover at most n points (one per column). But we have n lines, so total coverage capacity is at most n^2 points. But we need to cover n(n+1)/2 points, which is less than n^2 for n≥2. So capacity is not a problem. However, we need to ensure that each column's points can be covered by sunny lines. For column i, there are n+1-i points. If we have only sunny lines, each line can cover at most one point in column i. So we need at least n+1-i lines to cover column i. Since we have n lines total, we need to satisfy for all i: n ≥ n+1-i => i ≥ 1. That's always true. But we need to allocate lines to cover each column's points. For column 1, there are n points. So we need at least n lines to cover column 1 if we only use sunny lines. Since we have exactly n lines, we would need each line to cover exactly one point in column 1. That means each line must intersect column 1 at a distinct point (i.e., each line passes through a distinct point (1,b) for b=1,...,n). So we need n lines each passing through a distinct point (1,b). That's possible: we can take lines through (1,b) with slopes chosen arbitrarily (except 0, undefined, -1). For each b, choose a distinct slope not equal to 0, undefined, -1, and also ensure lines are distinct (they will be). Then each line will intersect column 1 at its designated point, and also intersect other columns at some points (mayb