lowed. So S2: y = x + 2 (passes through (1,3),(2,4),(3,5) not needed). So S2 covers (1,3),(2,4).\n\nLine S3: maybe through (2,2) and (3,3) slope = (3-2)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. So S3: y = x (passes through (2,2),(3,3),(1,1) already covered, (4,4) not needed). So S3 covers (2,2),(3,3).\n\nLine S4: remaining points: (3,2),(4,2). These are (3,2) and (4,2) share same y coordinate, horizontal line y=2, not allowed. So we cannot cover both with a sunny line. We need to assign them to different lines, but we have only one line left. So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nWe could try to assign (3,2) to one of the existing lines. Let's see if any of S1,S2,S3 can be modified to include (3,2). S1: y = x + 1 passes through (3,4), not (3,2). S2: y = x + 2 passes through (3,5), not (3,2). S3: y = x passes through (3,3), not (3,2). So none cover (3,2). Could we modify S1 to be a line through (1,2) and (3,2)? That would be horizontal y=2, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nMaybe we can use a different line for (3,2) and (4,2) that is not horizontal. For example, line through (3,2) and (4,2) is horizontal, not allowed. So we need to cover them separately. So we need at least two lines for (3,2) and (4,2). But we have only 4 lines total, and we already used 3 lines for other points. So we need to adjust to use a line that covers (3,2) and also some other point, freeing up a line for (4,2). Let's try to incorporate (3,2) into S1 or S2 or S3.\n\nConsider S1: we could change S1 to be line through (1,2) and (3,2). That's horizontal, not allowed. So no.\n\nConsider S2: line through (1,3) and (3,2) slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. So we could define S2 as line through (1,3) and (3,2). Let's compute: slope = -0.5, equation: y - 3 = -0.5 (x - 1) => y = -0.5 x + 3.5. At x=2, y = -1 + 3.5 = 2.5, not integer. So S2 would not cover (2,4). So we need to reassign (2,4) elsewhere.\n\nAlternatively, we could incorporate (4,2) into a line with (2,4) maybe? Slope = (2-4)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cann