s that currently does not have a point from column 3. All lines currently have no points from column 3 (they have points from column 2 and maybe column 4 or 5). So we can add (3,2) to any line, as long as the line's slope remains not prohibited. Let's see if we can add (3,2) to L1 (which currently has (2,1) and (4,2)). L1 currently is y = 0.5 x. At x=3, y = 1.5, not 2. So (3,2) is not on L1. So we cannot add (3,2) to L1 without changing the line. But we could change L1 to a line that passes through (2,1) and (3,2) and (4,2)? Let's see if there is a line that passes through (2,1) and (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. Equation: y - 1 = 1 (x - 2) => y = x - 1. At x=4, y = 3, not 2. So (4,2) would not be on that line. So we cannot have L1 cover all three points (2,1),(3,2),(4,2). So we need to assign (3,2) to a different line.\n\nLet's try to add (3,2) to L2 (currently (2,2),(5,1)). L2 equation: y = -1/3 x + 8/3. At x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not 2. So (3,2) not on L2.\n\nAdd (3,2) to L3 (currently (2,3),(3,1)). L3 equation: y = -2x + 7. At x=3, y = -6 + 7 = 1, not 2. So not.\n\nAdd (3,2) to L4 (currently (2,4),(4,1)). L4 equation: y = -1.5 x + 7. At x=3, y = -4.5 + 7 = 2.5, not 2. So not.\n\nThus (3,2) is not on any existing line. So we need to adjust lines to include (3,2). Perhaps we can replace one of the lines with a line that includes (3,2) and a column 2 point, while still covering other needed points.\n\nWe need to cover (3,2) and (3,3). Let's see if we can pair (3,2) with a column 2 point. For each column 2 point (2,b), we can consider line through (2,b) and (3,2). Slope = (2-b)/(3-2) = 2-b. For b=1, slope = 1, allowed. So line through (2,1) and (3,2) is y = x - 1 (as we saw). That line also passes through (4,3) (not needed). So we could replace L1 (which currently is (2,1),(4,2)) with a line that covers (2,1) and (3,2). But then we lose coverage of (4,2). We could cover (4,2) elsewhere, maybe with L2 or L3 or L4.\n\nAlternatively, pair (3,2) with (2,2): slope = (2-2)/(3-2) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. Pair (3,2) with (2,3): slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. Pair (3,2) with (2,4): slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, allowed. So line through (2,4) and (3,2) slope -2, equation: y - 4 = -2 (x - 2) => y = -2x + 8. At x=4, y = -8 + 8 = 0, not needed. So we could replace L4 (which currently is (2,4),(4,1)) with a line that covers (2,4) and (3,2). Then we lose coverage of (4,1). But (4,1) could be covered by another line, maybe L3 or L2.\n\nLet's try to adjust systematically.\n\nWe have 4 lines to cover all points. Let's try to design them from scratch, using the constraints.\n\nWe need to cover column 2 points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4) each on a distinct line. Let's denote the lines as L_a for a=1..4, each containing (2,a). Additionally, we need to cover (5,1) (column 5), (4,1),(4,2) (column 4), and (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) (column 3). Each line can have at most one point per column.\n\nThus each line L_i will have a point from column 2 (2,i). It may also have a point from column 3, column 4, column 5, and possibly column 1 (but column 1 is covered by vertical line x=1). So each line can have up to 4 points (columns 2,3,4,5). Since we have 4 lines, we can potentially cover all remaining points if we assign each line a point from each of columns 3,4,5 (some lines may not have a point from a particular column). Let's try to assign:\n\n- Column 5 has only point (5,1). So one of the lines must contain (5,1). That line also contains its column 2 point (2,i). So we need a line through (2,i) and (5,1) with slope not prohibited. Let's compute slopes for each i:\n\ni=1: line through (2,1) and (5,1) slope = 0, not allowed.\n\ni=2: line through (2,2) and (5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3, allowed.\n\ni=3: line through (2,3) and (5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, allowed.\n\ni=4: line through (2,4) and (5,1) slope = (1-4)/(5-2) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus the line containing (5,1) must be either L2 (i=2) or L3 (i=3). Let's choose L2: line through (2,2) and (5,1). Equation: y = -1/3 x + 8/3. This line also passes through (3,? ) at x=3, y = -1 + 8/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not integer. At x=4, y = -4/3 + 8/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333. So it does not pass through any other required points. So L2 currently covers (2,2) and (5,1). It can also cover a point from column 3 or 4 if we can find integer point on that line. Let's check column 3: x=3 => y = 5/3, not integer. Column 4: x=4 => y = 4/3, not integer. So L2 cannot cover any other required points. So L2 will only cover (2,2) and (5,1). That's okay.\n\nNow we need to cover (4,1) and (4,2). These are in column 4. They must be on two different lines (since a line cannot contain both). So we need to assign (4,1) to some line L_i (i ≠ j) and (4,2) to another line L_j.\n\nSimilarly, column 3 points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) must be assigned to three distinct lines (since each line can have at most one point from column 3). So we need to assign each of these to distinct lines among L1, L3, L4 (since L2 already has (2,2) and (5,1) and cannot have a column 3 point because it doesn't intersect column 3 at integer point). Actually L2 could potentially intersect column 3 at a non-integer point, but we need integer points. So L2 cannot cover any column 3 point. So we need to assign column 3 points to the other three lines L1, L3, L4.\n\nThus we have:\n\n- L1: contains (2,1) and must contain one of (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) and possibly (4,1) or (4,2) (but each line can have at most one point per column, so L1 can have at most one point from column 3 and at most one from column 4). So L1 can cover (2,1) + one column 3 point + one column 4 point.\n\n- L3: contains (2,3) and must contain one of the remaining column 3 points and possibly one column 4 point.\n\n- L4: contains (2,4) and must contain the remaining column 3 point and possibly one column 4 point.\n\nWe need to assign (4,1) and (4,2) to two of the lines L1, L3, L4 (since L2 cannot take column 4 point). So we need to assign (4,1) to one line, (4,2) to another line.\n\nNow we need to find lines L1, L3, L4 that satisfy these assignments and have slopes not prohibited.\n\nLet's try to assign:\n\n- L1: (2,1) and (3,2) and (4,1). Check if there is a line passing through (2,1),(3,2),(4,1). Let's see if these three points are collinear. Compute slope between (2,1) and (3,2): (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, slope 1. Equation: y - 1 = 1 (x - 2) => y = x - 1. At x=4, y = 3, not 1. So (4,1) not on that line. So cannot have all three.\n\nMaybe L1: (2,1) and (3,1) and (4,2). Check slope between (2,1) and (3,1): horizontal, slope 0, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nL1: (2,1) and (3,3) and (4,1). Slope between (2,1) and (3,3): (3-1)/(3-2) = 2, line y = 2x - 3. At x=4, y = 5, not 1. So no.\n\nL1: (2,1) and (3,3) and (4,2). Slope between (2,1) and (3,3) = 2, line y = 2x - 3. At x=4, y = 5, not 2. So no.\n\nL1: (2,1) and (3,2) and (4,2). Slope between (2,1) and (3,2) = 1, line y = x - 1. At x=4, y = 3, not 2. So no.\n\nL1: (2,1) and (3,1) and (4,1) is impossible because (2,1) and (3,1) horizontal.\n\nThus L1 cannot contain both a column 3 point and a column 4 point simultaneously? Let's check if any combination works.\n\nWe need a line through (2,1) and (3,b) and (4,c) where b ∈ {1,2,3}, c ∈ {1,2}, and b and c are distinct columns (3 and 4). Let's find if there is a line through (2,1) and (3,b) that also passes through (4,c). The line through (2,1) and (3,b) has slope m = (b-1)/(3-2) = b-1. So equation: y - 1 = (b-1)(x - 2) => y = (b-1)x - 2(b-1) + 1 = (b-1)x - 2b + 3. At x=4, y = (b-1)*4 - 2b + 3 = 4b - 4 - 2b + 3 = 2b - 1. So the line passes through (4, c) if c = 2b - 1. Since c must be 1 or 2, we need 2b - 1 ∈ {1,2}. Solve:\n\n- If c=1: 2b - 1 = 1 => 2b = 2 => b = 1. So b=1, c=1. That gives line through (2,1),(3,1),(4,1). But (2,1)-(3,1) horizontal, slope 0, not allowed. So discard.\n\n- If c=2: 2b - 1 = 2 => 2b = 3 => b = 1.5, not integer. So no solution.\n\nThus there is no line through (2,1) and a column 3 point (b integer) that also passes through a column 4 point (c integer) other than the horizontal case which is prohibited. So L1 cannot contain both a column 3 point and a column 4 point. So each line L_i (i=1,3,4) can contain at most one of the column 3 or column 4 points, not both. Since we have 3 lines (L1, L3, L4) and need to cover 3 column 3 points and 2 column 4 points, we need to assign column 4 points to two of the three lines, and the remaining line will cover a column 3 point only. But each line can cover at most one column 3 point, so we need to assign each column 3 point to a distinct line. So we have 3 column 3 points and 3 lines, so each line must cover exactly one column 3 point. Then we have 2 column 4 points to assign to two of the three lines, meaning two lines will have both a column 3 point and a column 4 point, which we just argued is impossible. So it's impossible to have a configuration with vertical line x=1 and 4 sunny lines covering the remaining points. Therefore k=4 is impossible for n=5 with vertical line x=1 as the non-sunny line.\n\nBut perhaps we could choose a different non-sunny line, not vertical x=1. Let's try a horizontal line y=1 as the non-sunny line, as we attempted earlier. That left column 1 points uncovered. We saw that we need to cover column 1 points (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) with 4 sunny lines, each also covering other points. That also seemed impossible due to constraints.\n\nAlternatively, we could use a diagonal line x+y = c as the non-sunny line. For n=5, we could use diagonal x+y = 2 (covers (1,1)), x+y = 3 (covers (1,2),(2,1)), etc. But we need a diagonal line that covers many points. The diagonal line x+y = 6 (c=6) covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). That's 5 points, which is a lot. That diagonal line is non-sunny (parallel to x+y=0). So we could use that as the non-sunny line, covering many points. Then we need to cover the remaining points with 4 sunny lines.\n\nLet's try that: Use diagonal line D: x+y = 6 (non-sunny). This covers points: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Good. Now we need to cover the remaining points with 4 sunny lines. The remaining points are those with a+b ≤ 6 but not on D:\n\nList all points again:\n\nSum=2: (1,1)\nSum=3: (1,2),(2,1)\nSum=4: (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\nSum=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\nSum=6: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1) (covered by D)\n\nSo remaining points are:\n\n(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1).\n\nThat's 10 points. We have 4 sunny lines to cover them. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most one point. Let's see column distribution:\n\nColumn 1: points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) (4 points)\nColumn 2: points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) (3 points)\nColumn 3: points (3,1),(3,2) (2 points)\nColumn 4: point (4,1) (1 point)\n\nTotal points = 4+3+2+1 = 10. We have 4 lines, each can cover at most one point per column, so across 4 lines, each column can have at most 4 points covered. Column 1 has 4 points, okay. Column 2 has 3 points, okay. Column 3 has 2 points, okay. Column 4 has 1 point, okay. So capacity wise it's possible.\n\nNow we need to assign each column's points to the 4 lines, each line gets at most one point per column. So we need to assign the 4 points in column 1 to the 4 lines (one each). Similarly, assign the 3 points in column 2 to 3 of the lines (one each), leaving one line without a column 2 point. Similarly for column 3 and column 4.\n\nThus we need to find 4 lines L1-L4 such that:\n\n- L_i contains (1, a_i) for some a_i ∈ {1,2,3,4}, distinct across i.\n\n- L_i may also contain a point from column 2: (2, b_i) where b_i ∈ {1,2,3}, distinct across lines that have a column 2 point.\n\n- L_i may also contain a point from column 3: (3, c_i) where c_i ∈ {1,2}, distinct across lines that have a column 3 point.\n\n- L_i may also contain (4,1) for at most one line.\n\nWe need to ensure each line's slope is not 0, infinite, -1.\n\nWe can try to construct such lines.\n\nLet's assign column 1 points to lines:\n\n- L1: (1,1)\n- L2: (1,2)\n- L3: (1,3)\n- L4: (1,4)\n\nNow assign column 2 points:\n\nWe have 3 points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). We need to assign them to three of the lines, each line gets at most one column 2 point.\n\nWe can try:\n\n- L1: also (2,1)\n- L2: also (2,2)\n- L3: also (2,3)\n- L4: no column 2 point.\n\nNow assign column 3 points: (3,1),(3,2). Assign them to two lines that don't already have a column 3 point.\n\nWe can try:\n\n- L1: also (3,1)\n- L2: also (3,2)\n- L3: no column 3 point\n- L4: no column 3 point\n\nNow assign (4,1) to one line that doesn't already have a point in column 4 (none have yet). We can assign (4,1) to L4 (which currently has (1,4) only). So L4: also (4,1).\n\nNow we have assignments:\n\n- L1: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\n- L2: (1,2),(2,2),(3,2)\n- L3: (1,3),(2,3)\n- L4: (1,4),(4,1)\n\nNow we need to check if each line's points are collinear with a slope not prohibited.\n\nCheck L1: points (1,1),(2,1),(3,1) are collinear horizontally (slope 0), not allowed. So L1 fails.\n\nThus we cannot assign (2,1) to L1 if L1 also has (1,1). Actually (1,1) and (2,1) are horizontal, not allowed. So we need to avoid having any line contain two points with same y coordinate. So we cannot assign (2,1) to L1 if L1 has (1,1). So we need to adjust assignments.\n\nWe need to avoid horizontal pairs: any line cannot contain two points with same b. So we need to ensure that for each line, the b-values of points assigned are distinct.\n\nSimilarly, we cannot have vertical pairs (same a) but we already have at most one point per column per line, so that's fine.\n\nAlso we cannot have slope -1 pairs: any two points (a,b) and (c,d) with d - b = -(c - a) (i.e., b + a = d + c). So we need to avoid that.\n\nThus we need to find a set of lines satisfying these constraints.\n\nLet's try a different assignment.\n\nWe have column 1 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Let's assign them to lines L1-L4.\n\nWe need to assign column 2 points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) to lines, but avoid horizontal pairs: cannot assign (2,1) to the same line as (1,1) because that would be horizontal. So we need to assign (2,1) to a line that does not have (1,1). Similarly, (2,2) cannot be with (1,2), (2,3) cannot be with (1,3). So we need to permute.\n\nThus we can assign column 2 points to lines in a derangement relative to column 1 points.\n\nOne possible assignment:\n\n- L1: (1,1) and (2,2) (b-values 1 and 2, distinct)\n- L2: (1,2) and (2,3) (b-values 2 and 3)\n- L3: (1,3) and (2,1) (b-values 3 and 1)\n- L4: (1,4) (no column 2 point)\n\nNow check slopes for each line:\n\n- L1: points (1,1) and (2,2). Slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L1 is line y = x.\n\n- L2: points (1,2) and (2,3). Slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L2 is y = x + 1.\n\n- L3: points (1,3) and (2,1). Slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L3 is line y = -2x + 5 (since at x=1, y=3 => 3 = -2*1 + b => b = 5). So L3: y = -2x + 5.\n\n- L4: (1,4) alone, we can later add other points.\n\nNow assign column 3 points (3,1),(3,2) to lines, ensuring no horizontal pairs and no slope -1 pairs.\n\nWe need to assign (3,1) and (3,2) to lines L1-L4, each line can have at most one point from column 3.\n\nCheck each line's current points:\n\n- L1: (1,1),(2,2). Already has points with b=1 and b=2. Adding (3,1) would be horizontal with (1,1) (both b=1), not allowed. Adding (3,2) would be horizontal with (2,2) (both b=2), not allowed. So L1 cannot take any column 3 point.\n\n- L2: (1,2),(2,3). b-values 2 and 3. Adding (3,1) would be okay (b=1 distinct). Adding (3,2) would be horizontal with (1,2) (b=2), not allowed. So L2 can take (3,1) but not (3,2).\n\n- L3: (1,3),(2,1). b-values 3 and 1. Adding (3,2) would be okay (b=2 distinct). Adding (3,1) would be horizontal with (2,1) (b=1), not allowed. So L3 can take (3,2) but not (3,1).\n\n- L4: (1,4) only, b=4. It can take either (3,1) or (3,2) (both distinct b). But we need to assign each column 3 point to a distinct line, so we can assign (3,1) to L2, (3,2) to L3, and L4 remains without column 3 point.\n\nNow we have assignments:\n\n- L1: (1,1),(2,2)\n- L2: (1,2),(2,3),(3,1)\n- L3: (1,3),(2,1),(3,2)\n- L4: (1,4) (maybe also (4,1) later)\n\nNow we need to assign (4,1) to a line. It cannot be assigned to L1 because L1 has (1,1) (b=1) and (2,2) (b=2). Adding (4,1) would be horizontal with (1,1) (b=1), not allowed. L2 has (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Adding (4,1) would be horizontal with (3,1) (b=1), not allowed. L3 has (1,3),(2,1),(3,2). Adding (4,1) would be horizontal with (2,1) (b=1), not allowed. L4 has (1,4). Adding (4,1) would be okay (b=1 distinct from b=4). So we can assign (4,1) to L4.\n\nNow L4: (1,4),(4,1). Check if these two points are collinear with a slope not prohibited. Slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, which is prohibited (slope -1). So L4 cannot contain both (1,4) and (4,1). So we need to adjust.\n\nWe could try to assign (4,1) to a different line, but all other lines have a point with b=1, causing horizontal conflict. However, maybe we can reassign some points to avoid that.\n\nAlternatively, we could assign (4,1) to L1 if we change L1's points. L1 currently has (1,1),(2,2). Adding (4,1) would be horizontal with (1,1). So not allowed.\n\nL2 has (3,1) already, so cannot add (4,1). L3 has (2,1), cannot add (4,1). So we need to modify assignments to avoid having any line with b=1 before adding (4,1). Perhaps we can assign (3,1) to a different line, freeing L2 to take (4,1). Let's try to reassign (3,1) to L4, and assign (4,1) to L2. But L4 currently has (1,4). Adding (3,1) to L4 gives slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed. So L4 could be line through (1,4) and (3,1). Let's compute equation: slope m = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5. Equation: y - 4 = -1.5 (x - 1) => y = -1.5 x + 1.5 + 4 = -1.5 x + 5.5. At x=3, y = -4.5 + 5.5 = 1, matches (3,1). At x=4, y = -6 + 5.5 = -0.5, not needed. So L4 now covers (1,4),(3,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to assign (4,1) to some line. L2 currently has (1,2),(2,3). It does not have any point with b=1, so adding (4,1) is okay (b=1 distinct). Check slope: line through (1,2) and (4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3 ≈ -0.333, allowed. So L2 can be line through (1,2) and (4,1). But L2 also has (2,3). Let's see if (2,3) lies on that line. Compute line through (1,2) and (4,1): equation y - 2 = -1/3 (x - 1) => y = -1/3 x + 1/3 + 2 = -1/3 x + 7/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667, not 3. So (2,3) is not on that line. So we cannot have L2 contain both (1,2),(2,3),(4,1) because they are not collinear. So we need to adjust L2's assignment.\n\nWe could reassign (2,3) to a different line. Let's see.\n\nWe have L1: (1,1),(2,2). L2: (1,2) currently, maybe also (2,3) originally, but we can change. L3: (1,3),(2,1),(3,2). L4: (1,4),(3,1). We need to cover (2,3) and (4,1) and maybe other points.\n\nWe have not yet assigned (2,3) after moving (3,1) to L4. So (2,3) is currently unassigned. Also (4,1) is unassigned. Also we need to ensure each line has at most one point per column.\n\nLet's list current assignments:\n\n- L1: column 1: (1,1); column 2: (2,2); column 3: none; column 4: none.\n- L2: column 1: (1,2); column 2: none; column 3: none; column 4: none.\n- L3: column 1: (1,3); column 2: (2,1); column 3: (3,2); column 4: none.\n- L4: column 1: (1,4); column 2: none; column 3: (3,1); column 4: none.\n\nWe need to assign (2,3) (col2), (4,1) (col4), and also we have (2,? ) maybe (2,?) we have (2,? ) left? Let's check all points:\n\nRemaining points after D (diagonal) are:\n\n(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\n(2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\n(3,1),(3,2)\n(4,1)\n\nWe have assigned:\n\n- (1,1) to L1\n- (2,2) to L1\n- (1,2) to L2\n- (1,3) to L3\n- (2,1) to L3\n- (3,2) to L3\n- (1,4) to L4\n- (3,1) to L4\n\nThus assigned: (1,1),(2,2),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(3,2),(1,4),(3,1). Unassigned: (2,3),(4,1). Also (2,? ) maybe (2,? ) we have (2,? ) left: (2,3) only. Also (4,1) only.\n\nWe have L2 currently only has (1,2). It can take (2,3) (col2) and (4,1) (col4) perhaps, as long as they are collinear with (1,2). Let's see if there is a line through (1,2) and (2,3):