mn, not allowed. So impossible. So this approach fails.\n\nThus we need a different arrangement.\n\nPerhaps we can use a horizontal non-sunny line y=1 to cover many points in column n and n-1 and n-2. Let's try a different approach: Use n-2 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-2, and use a horizontal line y=1 as a non-sunny line, covering (n-1,1) and (n,1). Then we have uncovered points: column n-1: (n-1,2); column n-2: (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). That's 4 points left. We have 2 sunny lines to cover them. Let's see if possible.\n\nRemaining points: (n-1,2) (col n-1), (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3) (col n-2). Two sunny lines can each cover at most one point per column. So across two lines, we can cover at most 2 points in column n-2, but we have 3 points there. So impossible. So that fails.\n\nMaybe we can use a diagonal non-sunny line x+y = n (covers (n-1,1),(n-2,2),(n-3,3),..., (1,n-1)). That covers (n-1,1) and (n-2,2). Then we have uncovered points: column n: (n,1); column n-1: (n-1,2); column n-2: (n-2,1),(n-2,3). That's 4 points left. Two sunny lines can cover at most 2 points per column, so column n-2 has 2 points, okay. Column n-1 has 1 point, column n has 1 point. So possible. Let's try to assign.\n\nWe have vertical lines x=1,...,n-3 (n-3 vertical lines). Non-sunny lines used: vertical lines (n-3) + diagonal x+y=n (1) = n-2 non-sunny lines. Then we have 2 sunny lines left.\n\nRemaining points after vertical lines x=1,...,n-3 cover columns 1,...,n-3 fully. So uncovered columns are n-2, n-1, n. Points in those columns:\n\n- Column n: (n,1)\n- Column n-1: (n-1,1),(n-1,2)\n- Column n-2: (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3)\n\nBut diagonal x+y=n covers points where a+b=n. That includes (n-1,1) (since (n-1)+1=n), (n-2,2), (n-3,3), ..., (1,n-1). So diagonal covers (n-1,1) and (n-2,2). So after using diagonal, uncovered points are:\n\n- (n,1) (col n)\n- (n-1,2) (col n-1)\n- (n-2,1) (col n-2)\n- (n-2,3) (col n-2)\n\nThus we have 4 points left, to be covered by 2 sunny lines. Each sunny line 